and Into the Spider-Verse was literally the best animated movie that I have seen in YEARS!

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.960
I mean sure, he got dusted out of existence and all that, (i don't feel so good)

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:07.860
So that was a bit of a low point,

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.209
but STILL, pretty solid year on the whole, and sure we may have lost one Spidey,

00:01:12.209 --> 00:01:18.960
Press F to pay respects,{ F} but heck Spider-Verse gave us another 6 to root for. They're multiplying like flies or

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.380
spiders.. I guess. You know what else it gave us though? An incredible tribute to the Sam Raimi Spider-Man trilogy

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.640
The one that redefined what superhero movies were in their darkest of days

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:29.440
(I was expecting bat-nipples)

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.680
The trilogy responsible for the superhero Renaissance. The movies that did the Thanos snap

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:40.679
17 years before it became a spicy meme. Sure, those references at the beginning of Into the Spider-Verse were just that:

00:01:40.959 --> 00:01:46.139
references to those past movies. This Peter Parker is not the same one as the one from those older movies,

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.660
but it did get me eager to go back and revisit that

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:52.169
original trilogy. For those of you who haven't seen these classics in some time,

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:57.149
you know the basic premise: bobblehead Tobey Maguire gets himself bitten by a genetically altered spider,

00:01:57.149 --> 00:02:02.549
"Great power great responsibility" blah blah blah your standard Spider-Man, but upon re-watching them

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:07.140
there was one mystery that was never explained in movies. One that has remained

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.080
unsolved for the last 17 years and a mystery that today

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:17.080
I think I can finally answer. You see in Spider-Man 2, largely considered to be one of the best

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.900
superhero movies of all time, Peter Parker loses his powers

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:25.860
(clip that shows movie plot logic)

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:26.380
(groans about the plot)

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:32.100
They just stop working. No explanation, no nothing. Just pooping out at the times that he needs them the most.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.940
Peter: Why is this happening to me?

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:34.960
MatPat: Good question Peter.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.200
That's what I intend to answer today. Taking this as a sign, Peter briefly gives up the mask to focus on his personal life.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:46.880
Later, when the threat of the super-powerful Doc Ock emerges, Spidey's dragged back onto the scene and wouldn't you know it, his powers just

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:49.468
conveniently come back the moment that he needs them.

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:52.349
And of course all this is probably just for plot convenience

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:56.279
But you know how I roll. If there's an explanation that goes beyond

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.739
"well, it was important to the theme of the movie", that I'm gonna find it, and I think I have. I believe that there is a

00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:03.639
legitimate, scientific

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:08.060
explanation for this seemingly random plot thread in Spider-Man 2. One that not only

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.620
fills the narrative gap AND gives us greater insight into the movie's themes,

00:03:11.639 --> 00:03:16.439
but also gives us a bit more information on the nature of Peter's spider abilities

00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:18.479
So to truly understand what's happening

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:25.829
We have to first rewind to Spider-Man 1. So in the first movie, running parallel to Peter's spider-puberty, is the story of Norman Osborn

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:32.900
His best friend's father, who does some sciencing on himself to gain laboratory induced superpowers with just a dollop of madness

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.100
*Osborn laughing*

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:42.800
As the newly-born Green Goblin, Norman does all the usual villain stuff: menace New York, turn people into spooky scary skeletons, dress as an

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:48.000
old woman, and star in terrible Broadway musicals. Oh man the stories I could tell you about that show

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:53.760
And yes that character with the inflatable t-rex coming out of his chest is The Lizard. "Give that man a Tony"

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:54.360
anyway

00:03:54.360 --> 00:04:00.940
The film comes to a dramatic climax when Norman drops both the tramcar and Mary Jane off the Roosevelt Island Bridge.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.840
Spidey manages to save them both - get DUNKED on Andrew Garfield!

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:05.340
*ded*

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.480
but in the course of the final battle, ends up killing Norman

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:08.980
Norman: "Godspeed, Spider-man"

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:09.500
Norman:"Godspeed, Spider-man

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:13.300
*Osborn in pain*

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.020
*ded 2: electric boogaloo*

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:19.640
Unsurprisingly this takes a mental toll on Peter. After Norman's funeral, he rejects the advances of Mary Jane:

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:23.340
Peter: "All I wanted was to tell her how much I loved her"

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:25.700
"I can't"

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:29.200
And listens to his best friend Harry swear vengeance upon Spider-Man:

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.340
Harry: "I swear on my father's grave, Spider-man will pay"

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:35.240
It's shortly after that that we see Peter's powers start to fail

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:39.380
So looking at the events of the first movie and how they impact Peter in the second

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:47.460
It's my theory that Peter's power loss is directly tied to him suffering from post-traumatic stress disorder or PTSD

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:51.660
Let's look at the facts. The anxiety and depression Association of America, or

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:58.709
ADAA for short, lays out four categories of diagnostic criteria for PTSD and Peter Parker in Spider-Man 2

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.859
qualifies in each and every one. The first set of criteria relate to the trauma itself. To pass

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.860
diagnosis in this category a patient needs to have gone through one of the following: either directly

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:16.780
experiencing a traumatic event themselves, witnessing, in person, a traumatic event, or learning about traumatic events occurring to a close family member

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:23.440
Peter doesn't just hit one of these; he hits all three. In the first movie, Peter, in a matter of months

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:27.080
experiences two indescribably traumatic events,  (getting high?)

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:33.040
both of which involve the death of father figures. First Uncle Ben and then Norman Osborn, a man who claims:

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:35.040
Norman: "I've been like a father to you"

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.540
and not only to both men die, and not only does he witness both of those deaths,

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:43.930
but they are both dead BECAUSE of the action or inaction of Peter.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:49.479
He doesn't stop the robber, which directly leads to Sandman shooting Uncle Ben and after some brutal

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:51.520
psychological warfare from Norman:

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:55.800
Norman: "The Goblin killed. I have nothing to do with it. Don't-don't let him take me again!"

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:58.360
"I beg you, protect me"

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.100
Spider-Man jumping over the glider

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:01.860
finishes off his other other father. And all of this is without even mentioning his childhood love getting kidnapped because of her

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:02.360
*ded again again*

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:07.840
finishes off his other other father. And all of this is without even mentioning his childhood love getting kidnapped because of her

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:14.800
association with Peter. It's pretty much unquestionable that Peter has every reason to be suffering some level of PTSD

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.169
But is he really? Well, to know for sure

00:06:17.169 --> 00:06:19.478
we have to move on to the second category of criteria

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:26.859
which looks at any lingering psychological effects of the trauma and whether memories of it are intruding into everyday life. Of the following,

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:33.130
patients must be experiencing at least one to be diagnosed as suffering from PTSD. That list includes:

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:37.749
unwanted memories of the event interrupting the day, having dreams related to the trauma,

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.880
psychological distress when exposed to cues that remind you of the event,  or

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:49.660
physiological reactions to those same cues. Throughout Spider-Man 2 we see memories of Uncle Ben intruding into Peters everyday life.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:56.080
Ben's oft-repeated mantra "with great power comes great responsibility" drives Peter until he ultimately tells his uncle:

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:57.880
Peter: "you can't live in my dreams anymore"

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.220
We also know that Peter is having difficulty sleeping

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:04.300
He's suffering from repeated dreams of these traumatic events as he reports to his doctor

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:05.700
Doctor: "you say you can't sleep."

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:06.940
And Dr. Connors (supposedly the man in the live show with a t-rex in his stomach) says

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.136
"you always, appear EXHAUSTED"

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:13.080
in short Peter checks off each and every one of these qualifiers

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:17.040
and remember only ONE is necessary for a diagnosis of PTSD

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:24.460
the third criteria required for an official diagnosis looks at the trauma's effects on a patient's emotional life and self-identity

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:27.400
of the massive list that you see on screen right now

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:31.269
Only two are required to move into the final stages of diagnosis

00:07:31.269 --> 00:07:34.180
But Peter checks off a solid six

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:35.580
a SINISTER SIX

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:36.720
a SINISTER SIX if you will

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.120
if by sinister six we're replacing a man vulture

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.400
with persistent feelings of detachment.  
(wonder what super powers he would have)

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.200
Well the first two: negative beliefs about the world, and blaming yourself for the trauma

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:49.400
are kinda sorta...Okay, absolutely hundred percent true in Peters case

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:53.100
There's no doubt that he's isolating himself from the rest of the world as both

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:56.940
MJ and Harry complain about how hard it's been to see Peter

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:58.780
Harry: where you been pal? you don't return my calls.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:00.700
He's self sabotaging at his job.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:02.700
J.J.: Where ya been? been looking for ya all morning, why don't ya pay your phone bill?

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:05.500
Struggling to pursue the activities in life that he wants to enjoy

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.320
Dr. Connors: where are you headed Parker?

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:08.300
Peter: to your class

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.000
Dr. Connors: My class, is over

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:11.240
Peter: I am trying I wanna be here

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:14.560
And slowly eroding the most important relationship in his life

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:15.520
Mary jane: Do you love me?

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:16.380
Peter: I

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:18.200
Peter: ...don't.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:20.820
Missing Mary Jane's performances in her new play

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:24.400
and when he sees her pulling away into the arms of other people wouldn't you know it,

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:28.000
but that's when his powers truly start to falter.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.100
The powers appear to be tied to Peter's mental health

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:36.720
but to know for sure we have to move on to the final diagnostic category: "changes in arousal"

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:37.560
( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡°)

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:40.360
NO- NOT THAT SORT OF "AROUSAL"

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:44.920
the way you react to stimuli in the world around you and once again in this final category Pete checks

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:46.700
Practically every item off the list:

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:50.500
Problems with concentration, Problems with sleep, easy to startle, staying up all night

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:54.600
Unlike the first film, Spider-man becomes far more hyper vigilant in the second movie:

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.970
Staying up all night, listening to the police radio. Just hoping for something to come up

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:03.800
But where this theory really shines is in explaining why Peters powers come back.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.000
In the movie, they just sort of reappear.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:07.900
Mary Jane gets kidnapped, BOOM, They're back.

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:13.200
It is never addressed, but it can't just be Peter reacting heroically in the moment

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:18.410
He's already done that with a child trapped in a burning building earlier in the movie and his powers didn't come back then

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:22.879
No, there's something deeper here, something more psychological at play

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:26.000
You see, well...it's not perfect, and certainly not clinical,

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.900
Peter does manage to give himself some rudimentary PTSD therapy in the movie

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:36.710
After some advice from a very chill doctor: "You always have a choice Peter". Peter gives up his costume and starts focusing

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:39.799
On his normal life again. He gets his job under control

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:45.600
He's back to his studies full force, he re-engages with his friends again, seeing Mary Jane's play

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:47.400
reconnecting with Aunt May

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:53.290
Even reaching out to his neighbor in a B-plot romance that you think is going somewhere but then gets totally forgotten about despite her

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:57.800
Being totally available, totally into Peter, and totally making mean chocolate cake

00:09:58.140 --> 00:09:58.640
#

00:09:58.640 --> 00:09:59.140
#JUSTICE

00:09:59.140 --> 00:09:59.640
#JUSTICEFOR

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:00.460
#JUSTICEFORNEIGHBORGIRL

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:04.800
this behavior on the part of Peter Parker correlates to the type of work that you'd be doing in

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.480
IPT, or interpersonal psychotherapy

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.180
which specifically has the patient resolving lingering issues

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:14.400
with people around them in order to get more control over their life,

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:19.400
In order to attain a support network and ultimately come to handle their past trauma.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:21.159
through these interactions in the movie,

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:25.960
Peter is forced to confront some major questions about his relationship with Uncle Ben

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:32.000
and his behaviors as Spider-man. which in turn helps him come to terms with both Ben and Norman's deaths.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:36.700
Mix all of that in with a healthy amount of outdoor exercise and an overall healthier lifestyle;

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:40.400
both of which are regular recommendations for anyone struggling with a mental health disorder

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:45.860
and Peter has really done some heroic work. Only this time not for the city of New York,

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:47.660
But for himself

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:52.840
Finally after all these years why Peters powers disappear makes sense

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:55.900
He's dealing with the impact of emotional trauma

00:10:55.960 --> 00:11:01.320
Trauma that he never had the chance to properly address and deal with in his life when he was having to double as spider-man

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:02.340
(high-pitched scream)

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:02.980
*thud*

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:08.800
and the powers only return when he's able to step back, give himself the self care that he needs, and help himself

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:12.100
Before he reaches out to continue helping others.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:16.200
The saddest thing about this is that it's never explicitly addressed in the film;

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:20.500
that Spider-man, in addition to serving as a role model of physical strength,

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:24.620
could have also been shown as a role model of Psychological strength

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:29.199
of living with and handling not just goblins and demons out in the real world

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:35.500
But the even more dangerous and seemingly unstoppable ones that lived inside his own head

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:39.100
And at the end of the day this theory just goes to show that everyone

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:40.720
including superheroes,

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:44.900
have their own issues, and that battling against depression or the effects of trauma

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:49.060
Might not be as visually exciting as a battle against tentacle doctor over here

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:53.900
But it's a battle That is far more real, far more scary, and far more important

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:57.300
than any of the ones that you see happen in spider-man 2.

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:00.400
and that sometimes the most heroic action you can do

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:05.060
is Taking the time to rescue yourself before you try rescuing someone else

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:05.660
and with that

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:08.000
There are no more mysteries left in the Sam Raimi trilogy

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:08.500
well (oh here it comes)

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:11.580
Except for how a woman born in Brooklyn has a British accent (oh nvm)

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:15.620
Aunt may: its water over the dam or under the bridge or wherever you like it

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:17.480
I'm on to you AUNT MAY (if that is your real name!)

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:18.260
BUT HEY

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:20.760
THATS JUST THEORY

